# --- s011 Book I d.1 q.4 (= Ord. I d.1 p.2 q.2, nn. 77–158)
n("n259","s011-01","vtrum vltimo fine apprehenso per intellectum possit voluntas non velle","la","source","= *Ord.* I d. 1 p. 2 q. 2, n. 77 (*utrum fine apprehenso per intellectum necessario voluntas fruatur eo*). Gorris rephrases it in terms of the power not to will.","verified")
n("n260","s011-02","quodlibeto. 3° q. 57.","la","source","Henry of Ghent’s *Quodl.* III has fewer than thirty questions, so *57* must be a misprint (*q. 17*?). *Quodl.* IV q. 2 (*z* = 2) is not verified either.","uncertain")
n("n261","s011-02","prima secunde. q. 10. articulo p° et secundo","la","source","Thomas, *ST* I-II q. 10 aa. 1–2: the will is moved naturally to the last end, and necessarily by an object that is good under every aspect, the vision of God.","verified")
n("n262","s011-02","quodlibeto. 12. q. 5.","la","source","Henry of Ghent, *Quodl.* XII q. 5. Not verified.","to-verify")
n("n263","s011-03","quadrupliciter","la","source","Scotus’s fourfold distinction of the case (end apprehended obscurely in general or in particular; clearly seen with or without charity) structures *Ord.* I d. 1 p. 2 q. 2, nn. 82 ff.","certain")
n("n264","s011-04","Sanctus doctor tenet vbi supra","la","commentary","As in q. 5 of the Prologue (n197), *sanctus doctor* is Thomas, cited *ubi supra* (*ST* I-II q. 10 a. 2).","certain")
n("n265","s011-05","Aug. 13. de trini. c. 3.","la","source","Augustine, *De Trinitate* XIII.3.6–4.7 (*omnes beati esse volunt*). Scotus answers it in *Ord.* I d. 1 n. 152 (natural *inclinatio*, not elicited act).","verified")
n("n266","s011-05","non potest in suum contrarium quod est odire deum","la","commentary","Gorris’s gloss distinguishes the contradictory (*non velle*), which is in the will’s power, from the contrary (*odire Deum*), which is not, because evil as such is not an object of the will. The point is controversial: Scotus elsewhere allows *nolle* of God (cf. *Ord.* IV d. 49).","to-verify")
n("n267","s011-06","Qui te oderunt","la","source","Psalm 73(74):23 (*superbia eorum qui te oderunt ascendit semper*), or Ps 82(83):3. The gloss *oderunt i. noluerunt* is Gorris’s.","to-verify")
n("n268","s011-06","vt passiua et vt receptiua. vt actiua. et vt productiua","la","commentary","A fourfold consideration of the will (passive/receptive vs active/productive) that is not in the *Ordinatio* text. It anticipates later Scotist discussions of whether the will is necessitated in receiving the beatific act.","certain")
n("n269","s011-04","as far as it is concerned","en","translation","*quantum est ex parte sua*: as far as depends on the will.")
n("n270","s011-05","hating God","en","translation","*odire* for classical *odisse*, a late-Latin form.")
# --- B collation (full): B22R–B23R
n("n1045","s011-01","in contrarium est veritas","la","textual","B: *in contrarium est voluntas*, an evident slip.","certain")
upd("n260",append="B also prints *q. lvij.*, so the error is common to both editions.")
n("n1046","s011-03","voluntatem perfectam per habitum","la","textual","B: *voluntatem perfectiorem per habitum*.","certain")
n("n1047","s011-04","Ad secundum etiam dicitur","la","textual","B omits *etiam*.","certain")
n("n1048","s011-06","potest considerari quadrupliciter","la","textual","B: *potest capi quadrupliciter*.","certain")
n("n1049","s011-06","Primis duobus modis necessitatur","la","textual","B: *primis duobus modis potest necessitari*.","certain")
